{
    "_meta": {
        "id": "dusk",
        "name": "Dusk",
        "description": "The after-sundown look: light features on a dark ground — warm through-roads, tan trails, violet cycleways, deep water, a yellow marker.",
        "version": 1,
        "swatch": [
            "#FF5500",
            "#FFAA00",
            "#AA55FF",
            "#0055AA",
            "#005500",
            "#AAAAAA"
        ],
        "skin_note": "Presentation only, same rules as `default.json` (OBCC_Spec.md §5). The night inversion of the schema's field-guide look (epic #1016 §4): the cream paper becomes black ground, big fills go dark (deep-blue water, forest green, olive fields, a navy town lift), and everything a rider follows stays light — the warm through-road ramp is kept verbatim from the day skin because deep orange and amber already read on black, local streets drop from white to grey so a town grid does not glare, tracks lift from brown to tan, cycleways from indigo to light violet. Rail keeps its day values: black dashes on a grey base read as a grey dashed line on black ground with no edit at all. Buildings are dark grey — the quietest mark that still exists on plain black ground (a town without mapped landuse, e.g. central Monaco, would swallow black buildings whole). Every value lands exactly on the panel's RGB222 grid (4 levels/channel: 0/85/170/255) and every pair meant to differ occupies a distinct bucket; the catalog tests assert both. One more rule this document obeys deliberately: the bake merges features whose *schema* styles render identically (obc-pack's merge_fills/merge_lines retag merged geometry to one canonical style id), so feature types the schema merges — {unclassified, residential}, {service, pedestrian}, {track, path, footway}, {drain, ditch}, {wood, forest}, the grass family, the built-up landuse family — are restated with identical values here: a skin that split them would style only the canonical id's share of the merged geometry."
    },
